Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1: ADD/ADHD "is not one thing" like depression or anxiety, implying distinct brain "types" that his own ADD type test can identify
→ Unsupported — DSM-5-TR/ICD-11 do recognize different presentations (inattentive, hyperactive-impulsive, combined), but the specific multi-"brain type" framework implied here (and the associated proprietary test) is not part of any diagnostic system in the attached references

Claim 2: Stimulants shouldn't be given to everyone with ADD — "for the right brain they're miraculous, for the wrong brain they're a nightmare," which is why stimulants have a bad reputation
→ Partially supported — NICE does support individualized medication choice and notes that effect size, duration, and adverse effects vary person to person (1.7.21), and medication is tailored to individual needs (1.7.3); however, the specific "right brain vs. wrong brain" mechanism is not a framework found in NICE or ICD-11 — medication decisions there are based on trial response and tolerability, not brain "types"

Claim 3: Stop "medicating" ADHD with alcohol and marijuana because they "steal your dopamine"
→ Partially supported — the differential-diagnosis reference notes abuse of alcohol, nicotine, and cannabis is common among people with ADHD, particularly adolescents/adults, giving the underlying concern real grounding; however, the "steals your dopamine" mechanism is not clinical language found in any attached reference

Claim 4: Limit gadgets and video games because they "steal your dopamine"
→ Unsupported — ICD-11 notes ADHD is associated with increased risk of gaming disorder, but nothing in the attached references frames video games as "stealing dopamine," nor recommends limiting gadgets as a clinical intervention

Claim 5: Exercise should be recommended for ADHD (boosts dopamine)
→ Supported — NICE 1.6.1 explicitly recommends stressing the value of regular exercise for people with ADHD (the "boosts dopamine" mechanism itself isn't stated in NICE, but the recommendation to exercise is supported)

Claim 6: A higher-protein, lower-simple-carbohydrate diet is recommended
→ Partially supported — NICE 1.6.1 supports emphasizing a balanced diet and good nutrition generally, though this specific macronutrient framing isn't independently validated in the guideline

Claim 7: L-tyrosine ("elyroine") helps because it boosts dopamine
→ Unsupported — not referenced in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE; NICE's medication pathway (methylphenidate, lisdexamfetamine, atomoxetine, guanfacine) does not include amino acid supplementation

